1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,620
Step outside in New York tonight, and a glowing arch splits the sky in two.

2
00:00:04,620 --> 00:00:11,457
It rises from the east, arcs over the southern horizon, and comes down in the west, glowing with reflected sunlight.

3
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:16,349
And in the middle of it, there's a dark patch, as if someone took a bite out of it.

4
00:00:17,049 --> 00:00:20,639
That's what Earth would look like with rings like Saturn's.

5
00:00:20,639 --> 00:00:24,715
But move a few thousand kilometers and the view changes completely.

6
00:00:24,715 --> 00:00:26,479
In one city it's a thin line.

7
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:28,487
In another it fills half the sky.

8
00:00:28,487 --> 00:00:32,259
And in some places, the rings would steal the winter sunshine.

9
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:34,709
What If Earth Had Rings Like Saturn?

10
00:00:35,409 --> 00:00:36,801
First, let's build them.

11
00:00:36,801 --> 00:00:42,022
We'll take Saturn's main rings and shrink them to fit Earth, keeping the same proportions.

12
00:00:42,022 --> 00:00:46,374
The inner edge would sit about fifteen hundred kilometers above the ground.

13
00:00:46,374 --> 00:00:48,520
The outer edge, about eight thousand.

14
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:51,537
Edge to edge, they'd be wider than the whole planet.

15
00:00:52,237 --> 00:00:52,985
Why so close?

16
00:00:52,985 --> 00:00:55,573
Because rings can only survive near a planet.

17
00:00:55,573 --> 00:01:06,158
Inside a boundary called the Roche limit, the planet's tides pull harder on the near side of a clump than on the far side, so loose material can never pull itself together into a moon.

18
00:01:06,158 --> 00:01:11,910
For icy rubble around Earth, that limit is roughly twenty eight thousand kilometers from the center.

19
00:01:11,910 --> 00:01:13,866
For rock, about nineteen thousand.

20
00:01:13,866 --> 00:01:16,685
Our Moon sits more than twenty times farther out.

21
00:01:17,385 --> 00:01:19,940
And here's the part that's hard to believe.

22
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:28,200
Saturn's main rings stretch across roughly two hundred seventy thousand kilometers, but in most places they're only about ten meters thick.

23
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:31,230
That's about the height of a three storey building.

24
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:39,600
If you shrank the rings down to the thickness of a sheet of paper, by our calculation they'd still be almost three kilometers wide.

25
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:47,855
They're made almost entirely of water ice, in pieces from tiny grains up to boulders several meters across, all circling in their own orbits.

26
00:01:47,855 --> 00:01:49,962
Each piece is basically a tiny moon.

27
00:01:49,962 --> 00:01:55,290
Billions of them, bumping gently into each other, flattening the whole system into a sheet.

28
00:01:55,990 --> 00:01:57,022
Now let's travel.

29
00:01:57,022 --> 00:02:00,300
Postcard number one, from Quito, right on the equator.

30
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,853
You're standing exactly in the plane of the rings, so you see them edge on.

31
00:02:04,853 --> 00:02:09,467
Just a thin, bright line running from east to west, straight over your head.

32
00:02:09,467 --> 00:02:11,713
By day, you might not even notice it.

33
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:18,547
But the rings would still be up there, casting a narrow shadow, and a single stripe would cut across the equator.

34
00:02:18,547 --> 00:02:25,768
Every launch from near the equator, where rockets get the biggest boost from Earth's spin, would have to be planned around the rings.

35
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:30,180
Postcard two, from Mexico City, about nineteen degrees north.

36
00:02:30,180 --> 00:02:37,482
Here the rings become a vast ribbon, sweeping from the eastern horizon to the western one, high across the southern sky.

37
00:02:37,482 --> 00:02:45,272
All three main rings are visible, the faint inner ring, the bright middle one, and the outer ring, with a dark gap between them.

38
00:02:45,272 --> 00:02:48,315
The gap alone would look wider than the full Moon.

39
00:02:48,315 --> 00:02:57,139
At sunset, the whole arch would turn orange and pink, long after the ground had gone dark, because sunlight still reaches the ice high above you.

40
00:02:57,839 --> 00:02:59,657
Postcard three, from New York.

41
00:02:59,657 --> 00:03:02,567
The arch is lower now, and something is missing.

42
00:03:02,567 --> 00:03:07,962
The faint inner ring has slipped below the horizon, hidden behind the curve of the Earth.

43
00:03:07,962 --> 00:03:12,751
By our calculation, nobody north of about thirty six degrees could ever see it.

44
00:03:13,451 --> 00:03:15,145
Postcard four, from London.

45
00:03:15,145 --> 00:03:22,362
Now only the outer rings peek over the southern horizon, a low pale arc, like a permanent rainbow that never moves.

46
00:03:22,362 --> 00:03:26,379
And the farther north you go, the more of it sinks out of sight.

47
00:03:27,079 --> 00:03:31,139
And postcard five, from Reykjavik, at sixty four degrees north.

48
00:03:31,139 --> 00:03:31,654
Nothing.

49
00:03:31,654 --> 00:03:36,874
By our calculation, the outer edge of the rings would sit just below the horizon.

50
00:03:36,874 --> 00:03:45,896
People in Iceland, northern Scandinavia and Alaska would live on a ringed planet and never see the rings at all, unless they traveled south.

51
00:03:45,896 --> 00:03:52,920
Imagine flying from Reykjavik to Madrid, and watching a giant arch slowly climb out of the horizon as you go.

52
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:57,366
For many people, a first look at the rings would be a vacation photo.

53
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:01,528
The southern hemisphere gets the same show in reverse.

54
00:04:01,528 --> 00:04:06,849
From Sydney or Buenos Aires, the arch would hang over the northern horizon instead.

55
00:04:06,849 --> 00:04:10,503
Wherever you live, the rings always sit over the equator.

56
00:04:11,203 --> 00:04:12,356
Now the fine print.

57
00:04:12,356 --> 00:04:13,447
First, the nights.

58
00:04:13,447 --> 00:04:19,087
Ice is a great mirror, so the rings would reflect sunlight down onto the night side of Earth.

59
00:04:19,087 --> 00:04:21,148
Nights would never get truly dark.

60
00:04:21,148 --> 00:04:27,758
But around midnight, Earth's own shadow falls across the rings, and that's the dark bite you saw in New York.

61
00:04:28,458 --> 00:04:29,628
Second, the winters.

62
00:04:29,628 --> 00:04:37,989
Earth is tilted, so for half the year the Sun sits below the plane of the rings for the northern hemisphere, and above it for the southern one.

63
00:04:37,989 --> 00:04:41,322
In winter, the rings would stand between you and the Sun.

64
00:04:42,022 --> 00:04:52,001
By our calculation, at noon on the winter solstice, the shadow of the thick middle ring would cover a band from about fourteen to twenty seven degrees of latitude.

65
00:04:52,001 --> 00:05:00,266
Mexico City, Hong Kong, Havana and Miami would spend part of every winter in a long twilight, with the Sun dimmed or blocked at midday.

66
00:05:00,266 --> 00:05:03,817
Darker winters would almost certainly mean colder winters.

67
00:05:03,817 --> 00:05:14,102
And because the shadow drifts with the seasons, sliding toward the equator as spring arrives, every city in that band would get its own strange calendar of shadow days.

68
00:05:14,802 --> 00:05:15,925
Third, satellites.

69
00:05:15,925 --> 00:05:21,858
The International Space Station flies about four hundred twenty kilometers up, below our rings.

70
00:05:21,858 --> 00:05:26,041
GPS satellites fly about twenty thousand kilometers up, above them.

71
00:05:26,041 --> 00:05:32,473
But anything that has to cross the rings would risk smashing into ice at several kilometers per second.

72
00:05:32,473 --> 00:05:37,905
The space between fifteen hundred and eight thousand kilometers would be a no fly zone.

73
00:05:38,605 --> 00:05:41,348
And fourth, the rings wouldn't last forever.

74
00:05:41,348 --> 00:05:43,654
Saturn is losing its rings right now.

75
00:05:43,654 --> 00:05:49,326
Icy particles get electrically charged and pulled along its magnetic field into the planet.

76
00:05:49,326 --> 00:05:56,120
NASA scientists estimate this ring rain drains enough water to fill an Olympic swimming pool every half hour.

77
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:03,104
At that pace, the whole ring system could be gone in about three hundred million years, and maybe much sooner.

78
00:06:03,104 --> 00:06:08,817
Some measurements even suggest the rings themselves are young, only a few hundred million years old.

79
00:06:08,817 --> 00:06:12,074
That means we're lucky to be alive while Saturn has them.

80
00:06:12,774 --> 00:06:13,675
Now the twist.

81
00:06:13,675 --> 00:06:15,865
Earth may already have had a ring.

82
00:06:15,865 --> 00:06:29,520
In twenty twenty four, geologists at Monash University in Australia mapped twenty one impact craters from about four hundred sixty six million years ago, a time when there was a sudden spike in meteorite impacts.

83
00:06:30,220 --> 00:06:42,295
When they rebuilt where the continents sat back then, every one of those craters landed within thirty degrees of the equator, even though most of the land that could have kept a crater lay outside that band.

84
00:06:42,295 --> 00:06:43,345
Their explanation?

85
00:06:43,345 --> 00:06:50,636
A large asteroid passed inside Earth's Roche limit, broke apart, and formed a ring that slowly rained down along the equator.

86
00:06:51,336 --> 00:06:58,573
They even suggested the ring's shadow may have helped cool the planet, just before one of the coldest periods in Earth's history.

87
00:06:58,573 --> 00:07:03,173
Not every scientist is convinced, and the dating of some craters is still debated.

88
00:07:03,173 --> 00:07:09,512
But it means the view from your street might have looked like this once, long before anyone was around to see it.

89
00:07:10,212 --> 00:07:11,984
And Earth won't be the last.

90
00:07:11,984 --> 00:07:14,453
Mars's moon Phobos is spiraling inward.

91
00:07:14,453 --> 00:07:23,060
In roughly twenty to forty million years, according to a twenty fifteen study, it will cross Mars's Roche limit and be torn into a ring.

92
00:07:23,060 --> 00:07:25,591
Mars is about to become a ringed planet.

93
00:07:25,591 --> 00:07:29,199
On the scale of the universe, that's the blink of an eye.

94
00:07:29,899 --> 00:07:36,578
So, rings like Saturn's would hang fifteen hundred to eight thousand kilometers above us, only about ten meters thick.

95
00:07:36,578 --> 00:07:42,409
They'd be a thin line from the equator, a huge arch from the tropics, and invisible from the far north.

96
00:07:42,409 --> 00:07:47,220
They'd brighten our nights, darken our winters, and get in the way of our satellites.

97
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:49,768
And they might not be science fiction at all.

98
00:07:49,768 --> 00:07:51,296
They might be Earth's past.

99
00:07:51,996 --> 00:07:56,362
Next time, the fastest thing in the universe turns out to be painfully slow.

100
00:07:56,362 --> 00:08:01,761
Light takes eight minutes to reach us from the Sun, and the numbers only get worse from there.

101
00:08:01,761 --> 00:08:04,576
The speed of light is slow, and here's the proof.

102
00:08:04,576 --> 00:08:05,265
That's next.
